From 9a60db55f1f12f9e1403a7ace36528f260b11b11 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 14:18:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(bedrock): manage Character Creator characters

Read the five account recipes and native Dressing Room catalog so players can select a character, browse owned pieces, and edit saved recipes. Use the native appearance PUT flow with a profile hash check and a readback for slot, piece, color, size, cape, and emote changes. Keep the settings profile and unrelated recipe fields intact.

Create new characters from the nine bundled Bedrock 1.26.51 starter recipes, or copy an existing recipe into an empty slot. The bundled definitions supply complete default piece IDs and colors, so matching catalog pieces can be equipped without another saved character. Delete a character by saving its base-only recipe. Both operations follow captured native request shapes; deletion keeps another saved character active. The four-position emote wheel uses saved recipe entries and native empty placeholders.

Bedrock 1.26.51 cape captures on the DressingRoom_Capes Store page and the appearance PUT flow showed that an equipped cape uses the catalog pack UUID without a piece suffix and sits before the four emote entries. A direct authenticated account test confirmed that equipping a cape adds one mesh and texture to the assembled GLTF model; the account was restored afterward.

Sequential native Size captures confirmed four fixed height entry pairs. Changing height removes the previous pair and saves the selected pair before the emote wheel. A separate arm-width capture changed only the base arm field from wide to slim while leaving cs_arm as wide. The account was restored to its original recipe after these captures.

The account API returns recipes rather than a Bedrock skin packet. Fetch the active character model as GLTF, convert its meshes and textures to static Bedrock geometry and an atlas, and persist it for login. Refresh that model after active character edits. The service model has no animated face or body animation, so geometry and image conversion remain static. Resolve persona handles and tint groups from the complete recipe and current catalog, persist them, and include them in login claims. Built-in UUID types come from the native 1.26.51 Store pages and default pack metadata; height body and skeleton UUIDs match native Size captures.

A native 1.26.51 Mojang Office Shirt equip saved its owned pack UUID with no suffix. The login used that UUID as both PieceId and PackId, and the Store product UUID as ProductId, rather than the encrypted pack metadata piece_id. Free catalog pieces use /f, marketplace emotes use /e, and built-in pieces and emotes use /d and /de respectively. Preserve saved colors on category swaps and insert new categories before the emote wheel. The profile hash check and exact readback still protect each edit. Raw receipts, account profiles, keys, and captures stay private.

Decode owned persona assets using the current account's inventory receipt,
PlayFab GetPublishedItem, and the native libhttpclient CDN user agent.
Bound archive sizes and validate each manifest/content ID before decrypting.
Receipts and content keys stay in memory. Match animated geometry to the
character's body and arm sizes, replace its static preview surfaces, and
assemble looping strips for persistence, login, and live skin updates.

Private Bedrock 1.26.51 captures verified the owned Office Shirt pack and
the HelliArm's 16-frame strip and ten arm cubes. Keep those assets and the
account receipt outside the exported patch. Default face asset assembly,
free pack keys and tinted animated pieces remain
separate work; the model service still supplies their static preview appearance.

ModelBinary places the Steve face texture on a +Z head surface, while the
Bedrock 1.26.51 login capture binds that same face region to the -Z normal.
Reflect model positions, node translations, and normals across Z and reverse
triangle winding when exporting skin geometry. This preserves native skin
coordinates in both Java previews and packets instead of turning only the
menu camera.

Native Bedrock 1.26.51 HTTPS captures equipped HelliArm on the right and
then left. Appearance PUT saved separate pack UUID /r and /l entries;
choosing the same pack on both sides did not merge them into a bare ID.
Use those recipes for owned side editing, preserve the opposite side and
all tint metadata, and retain the emote wheel ordering. Packet handles use
LeftArm/RightArm and LeftLeg/RightLeg from the target protocol enum.
Filter animated bones through their limb ancestry so the unselected side
keeps its static geometry. Tests cover arms, legs, tint retention, separate
entries, removal, and side-specific geometry. The owned-arm flow has native
capture evidence; the analogous leg flow still needs a native write capture.
Free limb side recipes remain disabled until their encoding is verified.

Keep side selection visible at the default GUI scale. Compact wardrobes
use Minecraft's category cycle button, smaller thumbnails, and a single
row of emote positions; larger windows retain the full category list.
Reflow visible footer actions so hidden buttons do not truncate labels.

Load owned emotes through the same receipt-authorized pack path. Use their
pack UUID for the protocol identity and their named animation
source for playback. A Dressing Room preview plays on the active account
character without changing its recipe, with replay and stop controls. Keep
the native root/waist/body hierarchy in menu models and apply the same pose
to animated clothing surfaces. Reset authored poses before every frame.

Use Mocha 3.0.1 for actor-local Molang variables and frame queries, with
bounded AST size and nested-loop execution. Apply Bedrock's 1024-iteration
loop cap and a host execution budget. Interpolate pre/post discontinuities,
linear and Catmull-Rom tracks, blend weights, scale, and animation clocks.
Creator animation schemas and Blockbench's interpolation implementation
guide the sampler. Effects, delays, and entity-relative rotations fail
explicitly until their playback exists. Owned native 1.26.51 Battle Cry and
Kadoosh metadata resolve to 6.5 and 4.75 seconds; private asset tests sample
every frame at 60 Hz. A Java GUI recording verifies visible Battle Cry motion, attached clothing,
completion reset, and readable Replay/Stop controls at the default scale.
The fixture is offline; live account downloads still need verification.

Native 1.26.51 EMOTE and EMOTE_LIST captures confirm the pack UUID on the
wire, not metadata piece_id. Battle Cry sends 130 ticks and flags zero.
Walking clears PlayerAuthInput Emoting immediately; completion clears it
after 130 ticks. Expose those packets through the ViaBedrock provider.

Add a rebindable four-position in-world wheel with mouse and 1-4 controls.
Cache only entitled animations and advertise loaded wheel entries. Apply
one sampled actor pose to body and clothing through the authored hierarchy;
reset on movement, completion, disconnect, and account changes.
